What:           /sys/devices/platform/charger/charge_bypass
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        Tetris device maintainers
Description:
                Read or write the connection-scoped charge inhibition request.
                Accepts exactly 0 or 1, with an optional trailing newline.
                Defaults to 0 and resets to 0 when the charging manager observes
                cable removal. It is not a persistent setting.

                Writing 1 inhibits the primary charger and all present charging
                pumps and stops fast-charge algorithms. The independent vote
                does not change health/thermal votes or battery FET controls.
                The Basic policy restores the ordinary allowed input budget
                after fast-charge shutdown and refreshes it while requested.
                Source, negotiated USB/PD, thermal and known AICL caps remain
                in effect. This path never writes charging current or voltage,
                starts a fast-charge algorithm, or forces the input path on.
                An unknown input budget, allowance below the hardware minimum,
                or failed hardware transition adds a separate input-suspend
                vote. That vote is removed only after the charging latches are
                verified off and a valid current limit has been written and
                read back, or on release. The battery can supply the phone
                while input is suspended.
                Normal power-path arbitration still honours the other owners.
                An uninitialized manager returns -EAGAIN and a disconnected
                supply returns -ENODEV.
                Hardware failures are returned to the writer and the inhibit
                request remains set, preventing subsequent charging enables.
                Writing 1 again explicitly retries the operation.

                Writing 0 removes only this vote and wakes normal policy. It
                never writes charging-enable, power-path-enable, or an input
                current limit. Release remains available after a failed enable.

What:           /sys/devices/platform/charger/charge_bypass_status
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        Tetris device maintainers
Description:
                Read-only observation, formatted as one newline-terminated line:
                version=1 requested=0 online=0 active=0 power_path=0 charging=0 error=0

                All fields except error are unsigned decimal booleans, apart
                from version which identifies the format. error is 0 or a
                negative errno. Unknown charging state is represented by
                charging=1 and a negative error, never a successful observation.

                requested is the independent inhibition vote. online comes
                from the primary charger's power-supply attachment state.
                charging reports whether any present charger's charge-enable
                latch is set; it does not use conversion-running status.
                power_path reports the primary buck enabled and input high
                impedance disabled, read from the same hardware register.

                active is 1 only when requested and online, the input path is
                enabled, every charge-enable latch is clear, and there is no
                operation, input-policy or observation error. It does not prove
                electrical battery isolation or guarantee sufficient adapter
                power for the current system load. Another controller can
                suspend the input path, yielding requested=1, active=0,
                power_path=0.

                Disable/stop errors remain visible until a successful explicit
                retry, release, or cable removal. Transient input-policy errors
                are refreshed by the manager and cannot clear disable/stop
                errors. A pending PD contract yields -EAGAIN, an unrepresentable
                current cap yields -ERANGE, and a charging algorithm without an
                input-only callback yields -EOPNOTSUPP. Observation failures
                affect that read. On an older or unsupported charging driver,
                failed readback never produces active=1.
